WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.341
IPC message bodies may be text, binary records, structured objects, or streamed data.

2
00:00:05.341 --> 00:00:10.682
Whatever the format, parsing code should be designed as security-critical C++ code.

3
00:00:10.682 --> 00:00:15.613
A valid frame only proves that bytes were received in some shape.

4
00:00:15.613 --> 00:00:22.186
It does not prove that the message is semantically valid, authorized, compatible, or affordable to process.

5
00:00:22.186 --> 00:00:25.062
Length fields and boundaries need strict checks.

6
00:00:25.062 --> 00:00:29.171
Validate declared sizes before reading, buffering, copying, decompressing, or allocating.

7
00:00:29.171 --> 00:00:38.210
Track how many bytes are available, how many are required, and whether continuing would exceed message, record, nesting, queue, or per-connection

8
00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:38.621
limits.

9
00:00:38.621 --> 00:00:46.838
A parser should distinguish incomplete data, which may need more bytes within policy, from invalid data, which should be rejected.

10
00:00:46.838 --> 00:00:49.714
Avoid treating raw bytes as application objects.

11
00:00:49.714 --> 00:00:58.753
Direct casts into C++ structures can depend on layout, padding, alignment, lifetime, and endianness assumptions that are not necessarily part of the

12
00:00:58.753 --> 00:00:59.574
IPC contract.

13
00:00:59.574 --> 00:01:08.202
Convert bytes into typed values deliberately, check remaining bytes before every read, and validate ranges before using values as counts, offsets,

14
00:01:08.202 --> 00:01:09.846
indexes, or allocation sizes.

15
00:01:09.846 --> 00:01:12.722
Resource limits protect both availability and correctness.

16
00:01:12.722 --> 00:01:18.885
Bound message size, nesting depth, expansion behavior, queue growth, active requests, and memory per client.

17
00:01:18.885 --> 00:01:25.869
When parsing fails, preserve invariants, release partial resources, and avoid leaving the connection in a confusing state.

18
00:01:25.869 --> 00:01:31.210
Safe serialization is not just about decoding successfully; it is about failing predictably.

19
00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:39.017
Tests should cover truncated messages, oversized records, invalid encodings, unsupported versions, slow streams, and repeated parse failures so the

20
00:01:39.017 --> 00:01:42.714
receiver’s resource policy is exercised under realistic pressure.

21
00:01:42.714 --> 00:01:51.753
Monitoring should confirm that rejected data does not grow memory or queues over time during long-running sessions, retries, load tests, repeated

22
00:01:51.753 --> 00:01:55.040
restarts, reconnect storms, and client churn too often.
